{"id":"project-LICHEN-worker6-jo48","title":"[review] project-LICHEN-worker6-69p2: Ack::from_bytes panics on 2-byte complete ACK","description":"## Bug: `Ack::from_bytes_for` crashes on 2-byte input

**Location:** `rust/lichen-schc/src/fragment.rs:260-265`

`Ack::from_bytes_for` checks `data.len() < 2` at line 261 but then unconditionally reads `data[2]` at line 265. When a complete ACK is serialized by `Ack::write_to` (lines 224-234), it produces exactly 2 bytes: `[rule_id, (window<<7) | 0x40]`. Passing this back to `from_bytes` causes an out-of-bounds panic.

This is a 2-way round-trip break:
1. `write_to` for `complete=true` emits 2 bytes
2. `from_bytes` always expects >= 3 bytes (reads `data[2]` for `n`)

**Old code behavior:** The old `Ack::from_bytes` correctly handled 2-byte complete ACKs via early return at the `complete` check:
```rust
let complete = (data[1] & 0x40) != 0;
if complete {
    if data.len() != 2 || data[1] & 0x3f != 0 {
        return Err(FragmentError::MalformedAck);
    }
    return Ok(Self::new(rule_id, window, 0, true));
}
```

**Security impact:** Fuzzer input will trigger a panic, causing process abort in production. This is reachable from `receive_bytes` path in `FragmentReceiver`.

**Fix:** Add `data.len() < 3` guard before reading `data[2]`, and add complete ACK detection at the top of `from_bytes_for` that checks `(data[1] & 0x01) != 0` (the new complete bit position) and/or handles the 2-byte format from `write_to`.","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"bug","owner":"agent@lichen-project.dev","created_at":"2026-07-24T18:53:52.831730479Z","created_by":"AI Agent","updated_at":"2026-07-24T18:53:52.831730479Z"}

